title: Darwin App Sandbox Spike
status: implemented
created: 2026-10-31
author: adrian
context: "Litterbox confines agent commands inside an OCI container reached over the Docker API. On macOS there is no OCI runtime and no kernel namespace facility: docker, colima, podman machine and Apple container all boot a Linux VM first, which makes the container runtime a heavyweight external dependency for anyone who just wants to run agent-written code on their own laptop. macOS has a kernel-enforced per-process sandbox, driven by the sandbox profile language (SBPL) and applied with `sandbox_init` or the `sandbox-exec` CLI. It is not the App Sandbox, which only applies to a signed application bundle. `sandbox_init` needs no entitlement, no Developer ID, and no notarisation. The primitive has no supported API, Apple rejected the named profiles for SDK 27 and later, and the language itself is undocumented, so its longevity is a live risk. The prototypes live under this ADR directory and are discarded with it. This ADR scopes two proof-of-concept applications rather than a shipping feature: one built on the nono library, one built directly against the sandbox facility with a helper binary of our own. They are compared on quality, ease of use, and maintainability rather than on a metrics table, and the findings become the input to a production ADR. The existing Litterbox interface is tightly coupled to Docker and is not assumed to be the right shape; each prototype is written free-standing, and evolving the interface is deferred. Requirement titles are prefixed with the artefact they belong to (Shared, Custom, Nono) because the renderer has no nested headings."
problem: "On macOS, Litterbox cannot confine agent commands without a third-party VM-backed container runtime, and has no backend that uses the platform's own process sandbox. Before designing one, we do not know whether the primitive is usable for real development workloads, how much of its capability surface a library exposes versus the raw mechanism, or where each approach becomes awkward to own."
goals[6]:
  - "Build two proof-of-concept applications: one on the nono library, one on the raw sandbox facility"
  - Establish whether the macOS sandbox facility can confine real development workloads, and what a minimal usable profile looks like
  - Learn how much of the SBPL capability surface each approach can actually express
  - "Learn what the lifecycle costs: a process per command, pausing, killing a whole tree"
  - Produce a qualitative judgement of quality, ease of use, and maintainability for each
  - Keep the work small enough to throw away
nonGoals[11]:
  - Replacing the Docker backend on Linux
  - Running OCI images or installing packages inside the sandbox
  - A shared abstraction over the two prototypes, or any attempt to fit them to the current Litterbox interface
  - Sandboxing GUI applications
  - "Raising the isolation ceiling above the shared kernel: a kernel exploit escapes the sandbox"
  - Reaping orphaned processes that leave their process group
  - Changing the existing MCP tool surface
  - "Committing to one implementation before the prototypes have been exercised"
  - Designing a configuration interface, CLI surface, or integration with the existing Litterbox interface
  - Signing, notarisation, or packaging
  - Supporting toolchains other than Rust
requirements[23]{id,title,slug,description}:
  FR-001,"Shared: Fixture Application",shared-command-app,"A small Rust application is built, tested, and run by each prototype. It provides the realistic workload and the hostile workload in one tree, so both prototypes are exercised against identical inputs without a shared harness or shared types."
  FR-002,"Shared: Independent Prototypes",shared-independent-prototypes,"Each prototype is a separate binary with its own command-line interface and its own configuration. There is no shared trait, crate, or runner between them. Each is built the way its mechanism wants to be used."
  FR-003,"Shared: Fixture Scenarios",research-scenarios,"The fixture application covers a cargo build, test and run, and a hostile script that writes outside the workspace, writes to `$HOME`, reads a credential file, reads a keychain directory, opens an outbound connection, and repeats the write from a child process. Git, a dev server and a unix socket client were descoped and are recorded in findings."
  FR-004,"Shared: Workspace Materialisation",workspace-materialisation,"Each prototype materialises a working directory for the fixture application and uses it as the sandbox root. Upload and download are ordinary copies between host paths and that directory."
  FR-005,"Shared: Process Per Command",new-process-per-command,"Each exec spawns a fresh confined process image. The workspace and its scratch directory persist between commands; process state, pids and the rest of the host namespace are shared, because no mount or pid namespace is created. A command that stays resident, such as a dev server, keeps running after the exec returns."
  FR-006,"Shared: Process Group Lifecycle",process-group-lifecycle,"Each prototype establishes its own session and process group, and the harness demonstrates pause, resume, kill and workspace reclamation against that group. Exposing those operations through the prototype CLI was ruled out of scope, so supervision is harness-level evidence rather than a prototype API."
  FR-007,"Shared: Denial Diagnostics",denial-diagnostics,"Each prototype documents how an operator sees a denial: which command it came from, which rule produced it, and where to look. The quality of this experience is part of the assessment."
  FR-008,"Shared: Backend Selection",backend-selection,"The prototype is selected explicitly and is available only on macOS. The existing Docker backend remains the default and behaviour elsewhere is unchanged."
  FR-009,"Shared: Qualitative Assessment",qualitative-assessment,"The findings record, per prototype: the shape of its interface, how much of the fixture workload worked without special-casing, what the operator had to configure, what was awkward, and whether we would want to own it. Judgement is the deliverable; there is no scoring rubric."
  FR-010,"Shared: Findings Document",findings-document,"The spike produces `findings.md` beside the ADR: per question, the observed behaviour, the failure mode with the exact profile or error, and the open risks for a production implementation. Findings are recorded when discovered, not reconstructed afterwards."
  FR-011,"Shared: Spike Only",spike-only,"Spike code is additive and unreachable unless explicitly enabled. It is not wired into the MCP tools or the default CLI path, so it cannot be mistaken for supported behaviour."
  FR-012,"Custom: Helper Binary",confined-helper-binary,"A binary of our own applies the sandbox to itself and then replaces itself with the requested command. The main binary never calls `sandbox_init` and never calls `pre_exec`."
  FR-013,"Custom: Policy Ownership",policy-owned-by-the-helper,"The helper derives the SBPL profile from a small fixed set of primitive arguments, applies it, and execs. No intermediate policy document exists anywhere else, so profile logic lives in one execution context."
  FR-014,"Custom: Argument Transport",policy-transport,"Inputs reach the helper as primitives on the command line or a policy file it owns. A profile is never passed in from the caller, and the caller never emits SBPL."
  FR-015,"Custom: Maximum Granularity",policy-schema,"The prototype expresses egress as a port on loopback. The profile language accepts only `localhost` and `*` as a host token; `*` grants every host, so it is rejected rather than exposed. Unix socket paths and bind grants were descoped and are recorded in findings with the reason."
  FR-016,"Custom: Profile Surface",policy-surface,"The helper emits per-path read and write grants, a workspace, per-port loopback egress, an enumerated mach-lookup list, sysctl-read, and the credential invariants. Per-path exec, network bind and inbound, unix sockets, devices and pseudo-tty are deliberately absent and recorded with reasons in the findings."
  FR-017,"Custom: Invariant Rules",invariant-safety-rules,"The helper applies rules the caller cannot override: writes are denied outside the workspace and the additional write paths, reads are denied for `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, `~/.gnupg`, and `~/Library/Keychains`, and every network grant is explicit."
  FR-018,"Custom: Path Canonicalisation",path-canonicalisation,"Every path is canonicalised with `realpath` before it reaches the profile. Relative paths, paths containing `..`, and symlinks resolving outside the workspace are rejected, and a rejected path aborts before exec."
  FR-019,"Custom: Deny-By-Default Baseline",deny-by-default-baseline,"Profiles start from `(deny default)` and add a read-only baseline for the dynamic linker, the system library paths, the developer directory and the host toolchain store, which is discovered by failure rather than assumed. The helper sets `TMPDIR` to a directory inside the workspace and creates it."
  FR-020,"Custom: Fail Closed",fail-closed,"If the helper cannot parse its inputs, canonicalise a path, or apply the sandbox, it exits non-zero without execing. A command never runs unsandboxed because confinement failed."
  FR-021,"Nono: Capability Mapping",nono-capability-mapping,"The nono prototype links the `nono` crate, maps the same inputs onto a `CapabilitySet`, and applies it inside its own process. The mapping is written down so a gap is visible as a missing mapping rather than a mystery failure."
  FR-022,"Nono: Gap Inventory",nono-gap-inventory,"Every capability the fixture workload needs that nono cannot express is recorded with the reason: an API that does not exist, a rule that cannot be added, a Mach or tty access it will not grant, or a policy it insists on. Per-host network grants are expected to be the first entry."
  FR-023,"Nono: Platform Reach",nono-platform-reach,"The findings note whether one dependency could cover both macOS and Linux confinement, since nono also targets Landlock, and weigh that against the audit surface it brings."
nonFunctional[9]{id,title,slug,description}:
  NFR-001,"Custom: Audit Surface",small-audit-surface,"The helper is single-threaded, has no async runtime, no networking, and no dependency beyond libc. It is written from scratch because writing it is how the primitive gets understood; a few hundred lines one person can hold in their head."
  NFR-002,"Custom: No Forked Initialisation",no-forked-sandbox-initialisation,"Confinement is never applied from a forked child of a threaded main process. The helper is exec'd as a fresh process, which removes the unsafe `pre_exec` path and the hazards of calling into the sandbox library after a fork."
  NFR-003,"Shared: Primitive Longevity",deprecation-resilience,"Whether `sandbox_init` still links and applies on the target macOS, whether `sandbox-exec` still ships, and what each prototype would do if either disappeared. Findings record the macOS version tested."
  NFR-004,"Shared: Startup Cost Measured",startup-cost,"Applying the profile and execing is timed for each prototype against a trivial command, so the production design inherits a number rather than a guess. No target is enforced."
  NFR-005,"Shared: Reproducibility",testability,"Each prototype can be run again from the repository with one command and produces a comparable result. Fixtures live in the repository rather than in a shell history."
  NFR-006,"Shared: No Behaviour Change Elsewhere",no-behaviour-change-elsewhere,"Non-macOS platforms, the Docker backend, and the MCP tools behave exactly as before. The prototypes are additive and off by default."
  NFR-007,"Shared: Explainable Failures",explainable-failures,"Every failure is captured with the command, the profile, and the raw error or denial. A blocked command must be distinguishable from a command that failed for its own reasons before any conclusion is drawn from it."
  NFR-008,"Shared: Judgement Over Metrics",judgement-over-metrics,"The prototypes are compared on quality, ease of use, and maintainability. Quantitative observations are recorded as context for that judgement, never as a score that decides it."
  NFR-009,"Shared: Iterate Until Non-Start",iterate-until-non-start,"Each prototype is built as far as it can reasonably go. There is no timebox and no completion deadline. Whether an approach is a non-start is a joint decision; the agent surfaces a concern with evidence and continues unless told otherwise."
acceptance[14]{id,title,slug,description}:
  AC-001,"Shared: Write Outside Workspace Denied",write-outside-workspace-denied,"A command writing to a path outside the workspace and outside the additional write paths fails with EPERM, in both prototypes."
  AC-002,"Shared: Credential Read Denied",credential-read-denied,"A command reading `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` or any `~/Library/Keychains` entry fails, even when the workspace is inside `$HOME`, in both prototypes."
  AC-003,"Shared: Network Denied By Default",network-denied-by-default,"A command opening an outbound connection fails when no grant is given, in both prototypes."
  AC-004,"Shared: Grant Is Specific",network-grant-per-host,"With a grant for one host and port, that connection succeeds and a connection to a different host fails. Recorded as achieved, partial, or not expressible per prototype."
  AC-005,"Shared: Descendants Confined",descendants-confined,"A shell that spawns a child, which writes outside the workspace, is denied. Confinement is inherited, not per-command."
  AC-006,"Shared: Pause And Resume",pause-and-resume,"Pausing a sandbox stops its command from making progress; resuming lets it continue."
  AC-007,"Shared: Delete Kills And Cleans",delete-kills-and-cleans,"Deleting a sandbox kills the process group and removes the workspace directory, leaving no process or directory behind."
  AC-008,"Shared: Fail Closed",fail-closed-behaviour,"An invalid input, a missing or relative workspace path, or a symlink escaping the workspace causes a non-zero exit and no command execution."
  AC-009,"Shared: Docker Path Unaffected",docker-path-unaffected,"On a machine with the prototypes available, the Docker backend still creates, execs into, and deletes containers as before when selected."
  AC-010,"Shared: Questions Answered",findings-answer-questions,"Each goal is answered in the findings with evidence: the command run, the profile used, and the observed result, plus a written judgement."
  AC-011,"Custom: Applies Unsigned",profile-applies-unsigned,"A profile applied by the in-tree helper on stock macOS, from an unsigned local build with no entitlement and no notarisation, confines the process. Verified first, because every other requirement depends on it."
  AC-012,"Custom: Profile Off The Command Line",policy-off-the-command-line,"The profile is never an argument and never a caller-supplied file; a process table inspection during a command shows the workspace path and the command, never profile text."
  AC-013,"Custom: Golden Profiles",golden-profiles,"For a given set of inputs the emitted profile byte-for-byte matches its fixture, so a change to the emitter is visible as a fixture diff."
  AC-014,"Nono: Exercised",nono-conformance,"The nono prototype runs the fixture workload and the hostile workload, and its results and gaps are recorded."
edgeCases[12]{id,title,slug,description}:
  EC-001,"Shared: Process Group Escape",process-group-escape,"A command that calls `setsid` leaves the process group. It stays confined, because the sandbox is inherited and cannot be removed, but delete can no longer reap it. How often this happens with real tooling is recorded."
  EC-002,"Shared: Workspace Inside Home",workspace-inside-home,"The default workspace lives under the user's home directory. The workspace grant is a subtree exception to the home-directory write denial and must not widen to the parent."
  EC-003,"Shared: Symlinked Paths",symlinked-paths,"`/tmp` is a symlink to `/private/tmp` and profile matching uses resolved paths. A path that resolves outside the workspace is rejected rather than silently allowed."
  EC-004,"Shared: Login Shells",login-shells,"macOS terminals start sessions through setuid-root `login`, which a sandboxed process may never exec. Interactive shells that are not login shells work; `su` and `sudo` do not."
  EC-005,"Shared: Setuid Binaries",setuid-binaries,"Exec of a setuid binary fails with a `forbidden-exec-sugid` denial. No prototype is expected to grant an exception; the point is to confirm the error is legible."
  EC-006,"Shared: Primitive Removed",primitive-removed,"The header is deprecated, the named profiles are rejected when building against SDK 27 or later, and the language is undocumented. The Custom helper and nono both call `sandbox_init` directly and can declare the symbol themselves; if it disappears, the prototype reports unavailable rather than running commands unconfined."
  EC-007,"Shared: Toolchain Not Installed",toolchain-not-installed,"There is no image and no package installation. A command whose toolchain is absent on the host fails with the host's own error; the sandbox does not provide it."
  EC-008,"Shared: Dotfiles",home-directory-paths-in-the-baseline,"Tools read dotfiles such as `~/.gitconfig` and `~/.cargo/registry`. Reads of the workspace and of an explicit read-only list are allowed, so a prototype that needs them must name them; the credential denials always win."
  EC-009,"Shared: Limits Of The Mechanism",mechanism-limits,"Enforcement happens at resource acquisition, so descriptors opened before the profile applies keep working. There is no mount or pid namespace, no CPU or memory quota, no uid separation, no syscall filtering, and hostname rules match resolved addresses. Anything the prototypes appear to provide beyond this is a wrapper, not the facility."
  EC-010,"Custom: Sandbox Init Error",sandbox-init-returns-an-error,"`sandbox_init` returns a message rather than an errno. The helper prints it verbatim to stderr, exits non-zero, and does not exec."
  EC-011,"Nono: Breaking Releases",nono-breaking-releases,"nono ships breaking releases at a high rate. Each prototype pins one version exactly and records what an upgrade would cost; no dependency is upgraded as a side effect of an unrelated bump."
  EC-012,"Nono: Fixed Policy",nono-fixed-policy,"nono generates the profile from its own capability model. Where a scenario needs a rule the model cannot express, the workaround lives outside the library and is recorded as debt rather than hidden."
